Lab 9 schema migrations
=======================

Install dependencies:
    python3 -m venv .venv-lab9
    .venv-lab9/bin/pip install -r migrations/requirements.txt

Forward the cluster PostgreSQL Service:
    kubectl --context k3d-quickticket -n default port-forward svc/postgres 5432:5432

These revisions operate on the existing app/seed.sql schema.
The baseline revision intentionally contains no CREATE TABLE statements.

For an existing seeded database that has never used Alembic:
    .venv-lab9/bin/alembic stamp 9a0000000001
    .venv-lab9/bin/alembic upgrade head

For a database already managed by Alembic:
    .venv-lab9/bin/alembic current
    .venv-lab9/bin/alembic upgrade head

Do not stamp head instead of running the email migration.
Adding this nullable column avoids a table rewrite, but requires a short
exclusive table lock. The migration uses a 3-second lock timeout.
The connection URL contains credentials for this local lab environment.


Lab 12 bootstrap and rollback notes
----------------------------------
Existing Lab 9 databases must execute the Lab 12 sequence in order:
12a000000001 (concurrent index), 12a000000002 (expand),
Events Deploy A, 12a000000003 (backfill), Events Deploy B,
12a000000004 (contract). Do not upgrade directly to head while old
Events code is still serving traffic.

DATABASE_URL overrides alembic.ini for migration connections.
A PostgreSQL connection can be exposed with a loopback-only kubectl
port-forward. The lab uses lock_timeout=3s and statement_timeout=60s
via PGOPTIONS to avoid indefinite waits.

app/seed.sql now initializes the FINAL schema for a NEW empty database:
scheduled_at NOT NULL, nullable email, and idx_events_scheduled_at.
After initializing a fresh database and verifying that its schema matches
the migration head, run `alembic stamp head`, then `alembic upgrade head`.
Do not apply the final seed to an existing legacy database, and do not
stamp an existing database to bypass migrations.

The complete upgrade/downgrade/upgrade chain and final-seed bootstrap were
tested on separate temporary databases. The live quickticket database
was never downgraded.

A contract downgrade reconstructs event_date from scheduled_at, but does
not by itself make a production application rollback safe. Writers must
remain compatible with both columns during rollback; data synchronization,
constraints, indexes, and deployment order must be coordinated.
